1
00:00:02,134 --> 00:00:04,895
So we got started with Oak. We understand,

2
00:00:05,232 --> 00:00:07,239
hopefully, that it's about middleware and

3
00:00:07,255 --> 00:00:09,036
that our request travels through the

4
00:00:09,085 --> 00:00:11,123
different middleware functions and we can do

5
00:00:11,123 --> 00:00:12,584
something to the request there.

6
00:00:13,579 --> 00:00:15,361
Now that's nice, but it might not be the

7
00:00:15,361 --> 00:00:18,089
huge leap forward you hoped for.

8
00:00:18,796 --> 00:00:21,364
Well, we're going to make a quite big step

9
00:00:21,364 --> 00:00:22,038
forward now.

10
00:00:23,065 --> 00:00:23,322
The

11
00:00:23,852 --> 00:00:26,901
Oak module does not just expose this

12
00:00:26,901 --> 00:00:30,033
application object, it also exposes a router

13
00:00:30,130 --> 00:00:33,022
object or a router constructor function. And

14
00:00:33,022 --> 00:00:34,211
just like the overall app,

15
00:00:34,870 --> 00:00:36,974
you can instantiate this router.

16
00:00:37,681 --> 00:00:39,320
Now, what does the router allow you to do?

17
00:00:39,770 --> 00:00:42,550
The router allows you to create a special

18
00:00:42,566 --> 00:00:44,831
kind of middleware which you later can then

19
00:00:44,896 --> 00:00:47,547
hook into your app with the use method. You

20
00:00:47,547 --> 00:00:50,086
can use this router object to call various

21
00:00:50,198 --> 00:00:52,946
methods on it and you will have a method for

22
00:00:53,251 --> 00:00:56,074
every key HTTP method which is out there.

23
00:00:56,475 --> 00:00:59,119
You can add a GET method, you can add a POST

24
00:00:59,119 --> 00:01:02,693
method, a PUT method, a DELETE method, and

25
00:01:02,789 --> 00:01:06,138
so on. And these are all valid HTTP verbs,

26
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:09,647
so HTTP methods that can be sent for sending

27
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:10,994
HTTP requests.

28
00:01:11,779 --> 00:01:13,910
And if you, for example, use the GET method,

29
00:01:14,166 --> 00:01:14,711
you're saying

30
00:01:15,609 --> 00:01:19,054
I want to register a route for incoming GET

31
00:01:19,054 --> 00:01:19,583
requests

32
00:01:20,193 --> 00:01:22,023
And then you need to pass in an argument

33
00:01:22,023 --> 00:01:24,175
which describes the path for which this

34
00:01:24,272 --> 00:01:26,440
route should become active. For example,

35
00:01:27,002 --> 00:01:30,583
/nothing, which means every GET request to

36
00:01:31,097 --> 00:01:33,330
mydomain.com/nothing

37
00:01:33,908 --> 00:01:36,493
should now trigger the second argument you

38
00:01:36,493 --> 00:01:37,569
have to pass to GET,

39
00:01:38,244 --> 00:01:39,256
a function.

40
00:01:40,251 --> 00:01:42,644
And that is a regular middleware function as

41
00:01:42,676 --> 00:01:43,528
we have it down there,

42
00:01:44,122 --> 00:01:46,033
and you should therefore accept your context

43
00:01:46,113 --> 00:01:48,542
object here. You can also accept the next

44
00:01:48,542 --> 00:01:49,792
function here if you want to,

45
00:01:50,327 --> 00:01:52,146
but here I only need the context.

46
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:55,968
And now this function

47
00:01:56,628 --> 00:01:59,044
should be triggered for every incoming GET

48
00:01:59,044 --> 00:02:01,074
request to, in our case,

49
00:02:01,236 --> 00:02:03,548
localhost:3000/nothing.

50
00:02:04,713 --> 00:02:07,526
And here we can then set up a response and

51
00:02:07,558 --> 00:02:10,356
set the body, for example, again to some

52
00:02:10,356 --> 00:02:11,114
HTML code.

53
00:02:11,728 --> 00:02:13,908
We'll also refine this later with help of a

54
00:02:13,908 --> 00:02:15,875
templating engine. For the moment, I'll

55
00:02:15,875 --> 00:02:17,168
again hardcode it like this.

56
00:02:17,976 --> 00:02:20,642
And I'll set this to, let's say, an h2 tag

57
00:02:20,642 --> 00:02:21,095
where I say

58
00:02:22,744 --> 00:02:23,616
Course Goals.

59
00:02:27,370 --> 00:02:28,288
And below that,

60
00:02:28,917 --> 00:02:31,399
a form. So basically what we had before too,

61
00:02:32,317 --> 00:02:34,686
a form where we can set the action to,

62
00:02:35,379 --> 00:02:38,866
let's say, /add-goal and the method to POST.

63
00:02:39,964 --> 00:02:41,821
And in there, we can then have an input of

64
00:02:41,821 --> 00:02:44,665
type text with a name of new goal

65
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:47,489
and a button

66
00:02:48,296 --> 00:02:49,862
of type submit

67
00:02:51,137 --> 00:02:53,485
where we say add goal. And this will be a

68
00:02:53,485 --> 00:02:56,160
very ugly unstyled button, but it should

69
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:58,803
submit that form. And when the form is

70
00:02:58,836 --> 00:03:01,197
submitted, a request should be sent to add

71
00:03:01,229 --> 00:03:03,850
goal, and that request should automatically

72
00:03:03,866 --> 00:03:05,859
use the POST HTTP method.

73
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:10,027
And with that incoming requests to GET

74
00:03:10,402 --> 00:03:12,662
/nothing should return a response that has

75
00:03:12,776 --> 00:03:13,313
this body.

76
00:03:14,270 --> 00:03:15,077
Now we can also

77
00:03:15,738 --> 00:03:17,078
reach out to the router again

78
00:03:17,917 --> 00:03:18,224
and

79
00:03:18,983 --> 00:03:20,952
register a POST route to

80
00:03:22,034 --> 00:03:22,502
/add-goal.

81
00:03:23,180 --> 00:03:25,594
So now we're saying if a request is a POST

82
00:03:25,594 --> 00:03:28,924
request and it's targeting mydomain.com, or

83
00:03:28,957 --> 00:03:30,517
in our case localhost:3000/add-goal,

84
00:03:33,197 --> 00:03:33,449
then

85
00:03:34,099 --> 00:03:36,760
this function here should be executed.

86
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:41,395
And here we can then do whatever we wanna

87
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:42,643
do. For example,

88
00:03:43,177 --> 00:03:45,736
we can parse the incoming body

89
00:03:46,335 --> 00:03:48,004
by reaching out to context.request.body.

90
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:53,920
And body is now a method. And this method

91
00:03:54,097 --> 00:03:55,288
does something amazing.

92
00:03:56,029 --> 00:03:59,652
It automatically parses the incoming request

93
00:03:59,668 --> 00:04:00,892
body. Isn't that amazing?

94
00:04:01,476 --> 00:04:04,042
That means that now we don't have to do that

95
00:04:04,042 --> 00:04:06,913
manual parsing from before. Instead, that

96
00:04:07,089 --> 00:04:09,046
should give us some usable data

97
00:04:09,174 --> 00:04:09,912
automatically.

98
00:04:10,794 --> 00:04:13,424
We can just console.log the body here to see

99
00:04:13,905 --> 00:04:14,611
what we're getting.

100
00:04:15,364 --> 00:04:17,577
And thereafter, we might want to redirect.

101
00:04:18,251 --> 00:04:20,705
And on the context object on the response,

102
00:04:20,817 --> 00:04:22,613
there is a redirect method we can call.

103
00:04:22,966 --> 00:04:25,195
However, not if you're using version 4 of

104
00:04:25,227 --> 00:04:28,081
this package, as I realized. So I did remove

105
00:04:28,113 --> 00:04:29,186
the version number here now.

106
00:04:29,779 --> 00:04:31,653
As I mentioned, it's all under development.

107
00:04:31,974 --> 00:04:35,066
So only on the latest version, this redirect

108
00:04:35,066 --> 00:04:37,645
method is available. And to redirect, we can

109
00:04:37,645 --> 00:04:40,625
pass the URL to which we wanna redirect. So

110
00:04:40,625 --> 00:04:42,948
this is now also much easier than before

111
00:04:43,252 --> 00:04:45,720
where we manually had to set the redirect

112
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:46,104
headers.

113
00:04:47,178 --> 00:04:48,908
And with that, we're parsing the request

114
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:50,574
body and redirecting thereafter.

115
00:04:51,343 --> 00:04:53,714
And now we registered 2 different routes and

116
00:04:53,890 --> 00:04:56,518
all the heavy lifting and the overhead code

117
00:04:56,854 --> 00:04:59,806
of manually looking into the request method

118
00:04:59,838 --> 00:05:03,530
and URL and of manually parsing the body and

119
00:05:03,530 --> 00:05:07,110
of manually redirecting is gone. The only

120
00:05:07,142 --> 00:05:09,695
annoying thing is the HTML code here in the

121
00:05:09,695 --> 00:05:11,942
TypeScript file and we'll get rid of that

122
00:05:11,942 --> 00:05:14,912
later as well. But we already shortened this

123
00:05:14,992 --> 00:05:15,329
a lot.

124
00:05:16,212 --> 00:05:18,653
Now, the only thing to make this work is now

125
00:05:18,845 --> 00:05:22,393
that we need to make Oak and its middleware

126
00:05:22,393 --> 00:05:22,746
chain

127
00:05:23,356 --> 00:05:25,891
down there with the use method aware of our

128
00:05:25,923 --> 00:05:28,535
router because just creating such a router

129
00:05:28,647 --> 00:05:30,313
and adding some routes is not enough.

130
00:05:31,275 --> 00:05:33,935
Instead, down here I will remove these dummy

131
00:05:33,935 --> 00:05:34,832
middleware functions.

132
00:05:35,457 --> 00:05:36,242
I will now call

133
00:05:36,803 --> 00:05:39,351
app.use, and now we need to reach out to the

134
00:05:39,367 --> 00:05:39,767
router,

135
00:05:40,344 --> 00:05:40,777
and there

136
00:05:42,107 --> 00:05:44,078
we need to execute the routes method,

137
00:05:44,670 --> 00:05:46,545
and that will return a valid middleware

138
00:05:46,754 --> 00:05:47,811
which can be registered.

139
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:51,497
And we also need to execute the allowed

140
00:05:51,497 --> 00:05:51,897
methods

141
00:05:52,603 --> 00:05:55,477
method. And both these methods, routes and

142
00:05:55,557 --> 00:05:58,175
allowedMethods, return middlewares which can

143
00:05:58,223 --> 00:06:00,455
and should be registered with app.use.

144
00:06:01,258 --> 00:06:03,216
You don't need to call next anywhere here

145
00:06:03,522 --> 00:06:06,299
because I'm fine with just executing these

146
00:06:06,588 --> 00:06:07,584
standalone functions,

147
00:06:08,178 --> 00:06:09,751
but you could absolutely

148
00:06:10,330 --> 00:06:13,172
also add your own middleware before or after

149
00:06:13,380 --> 00:06:14,071
the router here.

150
00:06:18,918 --> 00:06:20,809
My middleware like this,

151
00:06:21,418 --> 00:06:23,166
and it will run besides the router

152
00:06:23,166 --> 00:06:25,266
middleware. You should add it before the

153
00:06:25,266 --> 00:06:27,606
router middleware though, unless you called

154
00:06:27,606 --> 00:06:30,139
next in your router functions. Here we're

155
00:06:30,139 --> 00:06:32,832
not calling next, so any middleware after

156
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:34,787
the router middleware here would not be

157
00:06:34,867 --> 00:06:37,609
executed. We would have to call next for

158
00:06:37,609 --> 00:06:38,330
that to happen.

159
00:06:39,308 --> 00:06:41,696
So here I'm using my own middleware,

160
00:06:42,257 --> 00:06:44,309
and if we now run this application

161
00:06:45,079 --> 00:06:46,794
It's getting compiled and executed,

162
00:06:48,013 --> 00:06:50,803
and it doesn't load anything because of

163
00:06:50,819 --> 00:06:53,593
course we should call next here in our own

164
00:06:53,641 --> 00:06:53,993
middleware.

165
00:06:54,747 --> 00:06:56,366
Otherwise, we never reach the next

166
00:06:56,366 --> 00:06:57,986
middleware in line, which is the router

167
00:06:57,986 --> 00:07:00,519
middleware, which would be nice to reach. So

168
00:07:00,519 --> 00:07:02,379
let's add next here in the custom

169
00:07:02,379 --> 00:07:02,732
middleware.

170
00:07:03,421 --> 00:07:06,083
And if we now reload, we see something, but

171
00:07:06,147 --> 00:07:08,873
this is not rendered HTML. This is just some

172
00:07:08,873 --> 00:07:09,290
text.

173
00:07:09,995 --> 00:07:11,775
But we can easily fix this by reaching out

174
00:07:11,775 --> 00:07:14,801
to the response And there we can set the

175
00:07:14,801 --> 00:07:16,156
response type here

176
00:07:19,011 --> 00:07:20,108
to text/html,

177
00:07:21,270 --> 00:07:22,803
and this will add the appropriate

178
00:07:22,835 --> 00:07:25,337
Content-Type header to the response. We

179
00:07:25,402 --> 00:07:28,162
could also manually set that Content-Type

180
00:07:28,162 --> 00:07:30,390
header, but using this approach is a bit

181
00:07:30,390 --> 00:07:30,759
shorter.

182
00:07:31,466 --> 00:07:34,519
And once we do that and reload, now this is

183
00:07:34,583 --> 00:07:36,640
getting rendered because now the browser

184
00:07:36,752 --> 00:07:39,468
receives the information about which kind of

185
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:41,126
data is attached to the response.

186
00:07:42,195 --> 00:07:44,631
Now if I submit this, my message,

187
00:07:46,534 --> 00:07:49,051
you see we again end up on localhost:3000,

188
00:07:49,930 --> 00:07:51,949
which is a good sign. It means that our

189
00:07:52,242 --> 00:07:53,480
redirection seems to work.

190
00:07:54,034 --> 00:07:56,984
And back here in the console, we also see

191
00:07:57,274 --> 00:07:59,876
that the body which we logged here seems to

192
00:07:59,876 --> 00:08:02,251
be a promise, and indeed it is.

193
00:08:03,415 --> 00:08:05,722
So let's actually await body here

194
00:08:06,569 --> 00:08:08,377
and therefore turn this into an async

195
00:08:08,377 --> 00:08:11,522
function so that we can use await to see

196
00:08:11,618 --> 00:08:12,756
what's inside the body.

197
00:08:13,493 --> 00:08:14,487
So let's save that

198
00:08:15,545 --> 00:08:16,571
and test this again.

199
00:08:17,709 --> 00:08:19,216
And now the app crashes.

200
00:08:20,130 --> 00:08:22,085
And the reason for the error is that now

201
00:08:22,197 --> 00:08:24,442
here we're using async await, which is

202
00:08:24,442 --> 00:08:25,307
absolutely fine,

203
00:08:25,980 --> 00:08:27,439
but now with our own middleware,

204
00:08:27,984 --> 00:08:30,741
we should definitely await next here.

205
00:08:31,992 --> 00:08:33,963
And therefore we need to use async here as

206
00:08:34,059 --> 00:08:34,188
well

207
00:08:34,797 --> 00:08:37,569
because if we don't, what happens is that

208
00:08:37,665 --> 00:08:40,613
next triggers the routing functions. One of

209
00:08:40,645 --> 00:08:42,872
those functions is asynchronous, so it

210
00:08:42,872 --> 00:08:45,211
returns a promise, but we don't wait for

211
00:08:45,227 --> 00:08:47,679
that promise to resolve, and therefore we

212
00:08:47,759 --> 00:08:50,242
immediately basically tell Oak that we're

213
00:08:50,339 --> 00:08:52,261
done and that it should send back the

214
00:08:52,261 --> 00:08:55,161
response when actually one of our router

215
00:08:55,225 --> 00:08:58,414
functions wasn't done yet. So we have to

216
00:08:58,494 --> 00:09:01,170
await next here so that all the next

217
00:09:01,234 --> 00:09:03,461
middleware in line, so all our router

218
00:09:03,461 --> 00:09:05,848
functions have a chance of completing.

219
00:09:06,362 --> 00:09:09,169
Otherwise, the response will be sent back

220
00:09:09,169 --> 00:09:10,018
too early.

221
00:09:11,253 --> 00:09:13,964
And with that, we cannot just extract the

222
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:16,545
body, but also we will be able to redirect.

223
00:09:17,091 --> 00:09:18,999
And now to extract the body properly,

224
00:09:19,657 --> 00:09:20,426
I will await here

225
00:09:21,020 --> 00:09:23,265
so that body now really is the extracted

226
00:09:23,265 --> 00:09:23,569
body.

227
00:09:24,387 --> 00:09:27,739
And in our case, when the type is form, as

228
00:09:27,771 --> 00:09:30,321
we can see, so if we send some form data

229
00:09:30,417 --> 00:09:32,069
because a form was submitted

230
00:09:32,828 --> 00:09:36,130
we will be able to get our value from that

231
00:09:36,130 --> 00:09:38,914
value object, which is basically of

232
00:09:39,060 --> 00:09:39,837
URLSearchParams,

233
00:09:40,970 --> 00:09:42,372
which is a browser API.

234
00:09:43,421 --> 00:09:44,759
And on the body value,

235
00:09:45,743 --> 00:09:47,323
we can then call get

236
00:09:48,081 --> 00:09:49,081
and get our

237
00:09:50,821 --> 00:09:53,093
new goal here because that's the name we're

238
00:09:53,158 --> 00:09:55,511
using up there. So we can get a value by

239
00:09:55,511 --> 00:09:56,864
that key with the get method.

240
00:09:59,381 --> 00:10:01,115
And I'll store it here in newGoal.

241
00:10:02,047 --> 00:10:04,375
And I will console.log newGoal for now to

242
00:10:04,439 --> 00:10:07,153
prove that this works. And if we now save

243
00:10:07,185 --> 00:10:07,474
that

244
00:10:08,630 --> 00:10:11,248
and we go back to localhost:3000

245
00:10:12,243 --> 00:10:14,267
and I add the goal finish the course,

246
00:10:15,278 --> 00:10:17,607
you see we are redirected. And here we're

247
00:10:17,623 --> 00:10:20,995
also printing finish the course. So now this

248
00:10:20,995 --> 00:10:23,693
really works. And this is now how we can

249
00:10:23,725 --> 00:10:27,013
parse a request body how we can send back

250
00:10:27,013 --> 00:10:29,304
responses and a redirect, and how we could

251
00:10:29,336 --> 00:10:30,794
still add our own middleware

252
00:10:31,323 --> 00:10:33,486
correctly with await if we got some

253
00:10:33,486 --> 00:10:34,864
asynchronous code thereafter,

254
00:10:35,408 --> 00:10:36,241
if we wanted to.

255
00:10:37,107 --> 00:10:39,462
Now that's Oak and the router in a nutshell.

256
00:10:40,055 --> 00:10:42,762
A lot of new concepts, but a lot of cool

257
00:10:42,778 --> 00:10:45,662
concepts that make building realistic web

258
00:10:45,662 --> 00:10:48,482
apps much easier because now we as a

259
00:10:48,482 --> 00:10:50,468
developer don't have to take care about the

260
00:10:50,468 --> 00:10:52,711
nitty-gritty details. We don't have to do

261
00:10:52,791 --> 00:10:55,323
all that parsing and all those if checks on

262
00:10:55,355 --> 00:10:57,747
our own. Instead, we just define different

263
00:10:57,763 --> 00:10:59,337
routes and what should happen in those

264
00:10:59,450 --> 00:11:00,960
routes, and that's it.

265
00:11:01,795 --> 00:11:04,285
Now, as a next step, let's make sure we get

266
00:11:04,285 --> 00:11:07,595
that HTML out of this and that we also get

267
00:11:07,595 --> 00:11:10,518
some dynamic content into that HTML code.
